1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,480
There are several ways to build this
function.

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:09,080
One starts with repeated small
multipliers: for a fixed number x,

3
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:14,680
take one plus x divided by n and multiply
that by itself n times.

4
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:22,573
As n grows, the result approaches
e to the x.

5
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:26,099
A second asks for the curve
whose height and

6
00:00:26,099 --> 00:00:29,200
slope match everywhere, starting at height
one.

7
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:34,292
A third adds a series: one, plus x, plus x
squared over two

8
00:00:34,292 --> 00:00:39,120
factorial, plus x cubed over three
factorial, and so on.

9
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:43,213
They meet because they describe the same
growth law.

10
00:00:43,213 --> 00:00:46,397
The series can be differentiated term by
term

11
00:00:46,397 --> 00:00:49,653
and gives back itself with starting value
one.

12
00:00:49,653 --> 00:00:54,853
Uniqueness of that initial-value problem
identifies it with the rate-law curve.

13
00:00:54,853 --> 00:00:58,133
The small-step limit reaches the same
curve.

14
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:03,653
These are three constructions of one
object, not three unrelated coincidences.

